18:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L20",null,{"guide":{"date":"March 6–7, 1862","place":"Bentonville Detour, Elkhorn Tavern, Leetown, Oberson’s Field, and Morgan’s Woods at Pea Ridge, Arkansas","result":"On the first day at Pea Ridge, the battle split into separate fights at Elkhorn Tavern and Leetown. The Leetown attack failed after Ben McCulloch and James McIntosh were killed and Louis Hébert’s isolated brigade was repulsed. Most of McCulloch’s division left the field without reaching Van Dorn and Price at Elkhorn Tavern","facts":[{"label":"Battle began","value":"March 7, 1862"},{"label":"Separate actions","value":"Elkhorn Tavern and Leetown, about two miles apart"},{"label":"McCulloch’s division","value":"About 7,000 men"},{"label":"Osterhaus’s first contact","value":"About 600 cavalry and three guns"},{"label":"Osterhaus before reinforcement","value":"About 1,600 infantry, nine guns, and surviving cavalry"},{"label":"Carr’s first reinforcement at Elkhorn","value":"About 1,250 men and six guns"}],"maps":[{"title":"Pea Ridge today","caption":"The preserved battlefield lies northeast of Bentonville. Elkhorn Tavern is on the east side of the park; Leetown and Oberson’s Field are roughly two miles to the west.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-94.09%2C36.41%2C-93.89%2C36.52&layer=mapnik&marker=36.454%2C-94.034"},{"title":"Two fights on March 7","caption":"Van Dorn and Price moved south on the Telegraph Road toward Elkhorn Tavern. McCulloch’s division moved east on Ford Road and encountered Osterhaus near Leetown. Blue marks United States positions and movements; red marks Confederate positions and attacks.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-3-leetown-battlefield.htm","image":"/images/pea-ridge-march-7-map.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the March 7 fighting at Elkhorn Tavern and Leetown with United States forces in blue and Confederate forces in red"},{"title":"How Van Dorn reached the United States rear","caption":"The National Park Service map shows the Bentonville Detour around Curtis’s prepared line at Little Sugar Creek. Federal roadblocks delayed the Confederate column and contributed to Van Dorn’s decision to divide it.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/bentonville-detour.htm","image":"/images/pea-ridge-bentonville-detour.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Bentonville Detour and the armies near Little Sugar Creek and Pea Ridge"},{"title":"Battlefield roads and terrain","caption":"This 1876 U.S. Army map identifies the Telegraph Road, Elkhorn Tavern, Leetown, Cross Timber Hollow, the ridge, fields, and wooded ground. Its troop positions depict March 8, but the terrain and road network also explain the divided fighting on March 7.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447263/","image":"/images/pea-ridge-battlefield-map.jpg","alt":"1876 map of the Pea Ridge battlefield showing roads, settlements, terrain, and troop positions"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:06","audioTime":66,"title":"Curtis waited at Little Sugar Creek","detail":"After Sigel escaped Bentonville, Curtis concentrated the Army of the Southwest behind a prepared position along the Telegraph Road. Van Dorn decided to move around it and sever the road to Missouri."},{"time":"3:31","audioTime":211,"title":"The Confederate night march began","detail":"At 8 p.m. on March 6, Van Dorn sent his exhausted army onto the Bentonville Detour. Many soldiers had marched more than 50 miles in cold weather during the previous 60 hours."},{"time":"3:48","audioTime":228,"title":"Van Dorn had left logistics behind","detail":"The supply train carrying tents, food, baggage, and extra ammunition had been ordered to follow the fast-moving army. Van Dorn also continued to assume that Curtis intended to retreat."},{"time":"6:17","audioTime":377,"title":"Cold and exhaustion broke down the column","detail":"Men and whole companies fell out along the detour. The army had little food and sleep after several days of forced marching."},{"time":"7:23","audioTime":443,"title":"The 4th Iowa blocked the detour","detail":"Grenville Dodge proposed obstructing the road as a precaution. His men felled trees at two places and delayed the Confederate advance for several hours."},{"time":"8:27","audioTime":507,"title":"Van Dorn reached the Telegraph Road after dawn","detail":"The head of the Confederate column entered Curtis’s rear on March 7, but much of the army remained on the clogged detour. Van Dorn decided not to wait for it to close up."},{"time":"9:06","audioTime":546,"title":"Van Dorn divided the army","detail":"McCulloch’s division, including Albert Pike’s Indian troops, turned east on Ford Road. Van Dorn and Price continued toward the Telegraph Road and Elkhorn Tavern."},{"time":"9:34","audioTime":574,"title":"Eli Weston guarded the army’s rear","detail":"Weston had six companies of the 24th Missouri and two cavalry companies around Elkhorn Tavern. His pickets reported Confederates on the Telegraph and Ford roads after sunrise."},{"time":"10:34","audioTime":634,"title":"The first firing began north of Elkhorn Tavern","detail":"United States infantry from Weston’s command met Confederate cavalry about three-quarters of a mile north of the tavern."},{"time":"10:51","audioTime":651,"title":"Curtis sent Osterhaus toward Leetown","detail":"Curtis first treated the movement on Ford Road as the main threat. He ordered Peter Osterhaus to take a division-sized force through Leetown toward Twelve Corner Church."},{"time":"11:54","audioTime":714,"title":"Curtis learned that Elkhorn Tavern was also threatened","detail":"Weston reported that Confederate strength was growing north of the tavern and requested immediate reinforcement. Curtis now faced two attacks about two miles apart."},{"time":"13:54","audioTime":834,"title":"Dodge’s unauthorized move put his brigade in the right place","detail":"After building the roadblocks, Dodge pulled his brigade from Little Sugar Creek toward the rear without orders. Curtis found it nearby and sent Eugene Carr with it to Elkhorn Tavern."},{"time":"14:51","audioTime":891,"title":"Carr reinforced Elkhorn Tavern","detail":"Carr and Dodge brought about 1,250 men and a six-gun battery. Carr placed artillery above Cross Timber Hollow and soon reported that the Confederate force was larger than a diversion."},{"time":"16:29","audioTime":989,"title":"Carr fell back to the tavern","detail":"By about 12:30 p.m., Confederate fire had disabled all but one of Carr’s first guns. Carr withdrew from Cross Timber Hollow as William Vandever’s brigade and an Iowa battery arrived."},{"time":"18:44","audioTime":1124,"title":"The episode shifts to Leetown","detail":"The Elkhorn Tavern fight paused while Van Dorn and Price moved troops through difficult wooded terrain. Two miles west, Osterhaus advanced to locate McCulloch’s column."},{"time":"19:24","audioTime":1164,"title":"Osterhaus placed infantry at Oberson’s Field","detail":"Osterhaus left his infantry and artillery along the south edge of the field. He took about 600 cavalrymen and three guns north to scout beyond the trees."},{"time":"20:57","audioTime":1257,"title":"Osterhaus found McCulloch’s division","detail":"Beyond the trees, the United States cavalry saw about 7,000 Confederates marching east on Ford Road. The head of the column was close to Curtis’s headquarters and wagon park."},{"time":"22:01","audioTime":1321,"title":"Elbert’s battery opened fire","detail":"Osterhaus ordered three James rifles to fire on the marching column. The attack delayed McCulloch and protected the United States right flank until reinforcements could arrive."},{"time":"22:24","audioTime":1344,"title":"Confederate cavalry overran the guns","detail":"James McIntosh sent about 3,000 Texas and Arkansas horsemen against roughly 600 United States cavalrymen. The charge captured the three guns and scattered their supports."},{"time":"24:16","audioTime":1456,"title":"Pike’s troops struck two Iowa cavalry companies","detail":"Companies A and B of the 3rd Iowa Cavalry encountered Creek and Cherokee troops near Foster Lane. Private Albert Power returned under fire to rescue a dismounted comrade and later received the Medal of Honor."},{"time":"25:17","audioTime":1517,"title":"The United States cavalry retreated to Oberson’s Field","detail":"Nicholas Greusel kept his infantry steady along the south edge of the field while cavalrymen and loose horses passed through the line."},{"time":"27:02","audioTime":1622,"title":"McCulloch stopped his march and formed for attack","detail":"Saul Ross reported a substantial United States line at Oberson’s Field. McCulloch deployed infantry on both sides of Leetown Road, with McIntosh’s cavalry and Pike’s troops behind it."},{"time":"29:28","audioTime":1768,"title":"McCulloch rode forward to reconnoiter","detail":"At about 1:30 p.m., McCulloch approached the north edge of Oberson’s Field alone. Skirmishers from the 36th Illinois were hidden along the fence line roughly 70 yards away."},{"time":"30:36","audioTime":1836,"title":"United States skirmishers killed McCulloch","detail":"A volley struck McCulloch, including a fatal shot through the chest. His staff did not see him fall, and the loss was not immediately communicated across the division."},{"time":"31:12","audioTime":1872,"title":"McIntosh took command and was also killed","detail":"McIntosh advanced with the 2nd Arkansas Mounted Rifles instead of launching the planned general attack. United States fire killed him within minutes of McCulloch."},{"time":"31:46","audioTime":1906,"title":"Command failed west of Leetown Road","detail":"Regimental commanders halted and waited for orders. Senior surviving officer Louis Hébert was in Morgan’s Woods and did not know that command had passed to him."},{"time":"32:35","audioTime":1955,"title":"Hébert attacked through Morgan’s Woods","detail":"Hébert’s four regiments moved against Osterhaus’s right. Thick woods disrupted the Confederate line and gave Jefferson C. Davis enough time to arrive with reinforcements."},{"time":"33:25","audioTime":2005,"title":"Davis and Hébert fought at close range","detail":"The two forces fought in dense brush for almost an hour. Davis withdrew to Oberson’s Field around 3 p.m., and Hébert’s men followed into United States artillery fire."},{"time":"34:05","audioTime":2045,"title":"The supporting Confederate attack never came","detail":"Five Arkansas, six Texas, and two Indian regiments west of Leetown Road remained idle after losing their commanders. Hébert’s unsupported troops withdrew into Morgan’s Woods."},{"time":"34:38","audioTime":2078,"title":"McCulloch’s division broke apart","detail":"The Leetown fighting ended with the Confederate division fragmented and moving away in several directions. Most of half of Van Dorn’s army was no longer available to support the continuing attack at Elkhorn Tavern."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Samuel R. Curtis","command":"Army of the Southwest","subordinates":"Directed both separated defensive actions"},{"commander":"Eugene A. Carr","command":"4th Division at Elkhorn Tavern","subordinates":"Grenville Dodge and William Vandever"},{"commander":"Eli Weston","command":"Rear-area detachment at Elkhorn Tavern","subordinates":"24th Missouri companies and cavalry"},{"commander":"Peter J. Osterhaus","command":"Leetown force","subordinates":"Nicholas Greusel, cavalry, and artillery"},{"commander":"Jefferson C. Davis","command":"3rd Division","subordinates":"Reinforced Osterhaus at Morgan’s Woods"},{"commander":"Nicholas Greusel","command":"Infantry at Oberson’s Field","subordinates":"36th Illinois and other regiments"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Earl Van Dorn","command":"Army of the West","subordinates":"Overall command; accompanied Price at Elkhorn Tavern"},{"commander":"Sterling Price","command":"Missouri State Guard at Elkhorn Tavern","subordinates":"Advanced south on the Telegraph Road"},{"commander":"Benjamin McCulloch","command":"McCulloch’s Division at Leetown","subordinates":"Killed before the planned general attack"},{"commander":"James McIntosh","command":"Cavalry and successor to McCulloch","subordinates":"Killed shortly after assuming command"},{"commander":"Louis Hébert","command":"Infantry brigade in Morgan’s Woods","subordinates":"Senior surviving officer but unaware that division command had passed to him"},{"commander":"Albert Pike","command":"Indian Territory brigade","subordinates":"Creek and Cherokee regiments behind McIntosh"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Benjamin McCulloch","role":"Commanded the Confederate division at Leetown and was killed while reconnoitering the United States line","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ben-mcculloch.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/general-mcculloch.htm"},{"name":"Louis Hébert","role":"Led four Confederate regiments through Morgan’s Woods and became the division’s senior surviving officer","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/louis-hebert.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/colonel-hebert.htm"},{"name":"Samuel R. Curtis","role":"Responded to attacks in the army’s rear by sending forces to both Elkhorn Tavern and Leetown","side":"United States","image":"/images/samuel-r-curtis.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018669095/"},{"name":"Earl Van Dorn","role":"Divided the Army of the West and remained with Price at Elkhorn Tavern","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/earl-van-dorn.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/general-van-dorn.htm"},{"name":"Eugene A. Carr","role":"Commanded the United States defense around Elkhorn Tavern","side":"United States"},{"name":"Peter J. Osterhaus","role":"Found and delayed McCulloch’s division near Leetown","side":"United States"},{"name":"James McIntosh","role":"Led the cavalry charge that captured Elbert’s guns and briefly succeeded McCulloch","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Grenville M. Dodge","role":"Blocked the Bentonville Detour and moved his brigade toward the army’s rear before receiving orders","side":"United States"},{"name":"Albert Power","role":"3rd Iowa cavalryman who rescued a dismounted comrade and later received the Medal of Honor","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Benjamin McCulloch","side":"Confederacy","role":"Division commander at Leetown","outcome":"Killed instantly by United States skirmishers while reconnoitering ahead of the 16th Arkansas."},{"name":"James McIntosh","side":"Confederacy","role":"Cavalry commander and McCulloch’s successor","outcome":"Killed while advancing with the 2nd Arkansas Mounted Rifles minutes after McCulloch’s death."},{"name":"Eugene A. Carr","side":"United States","role":"Commander of the 4th Division at Elkhorn Tavern","outcome":"Wounded three times during the March 7 fighting; remained in command."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-oberson-field.jpg","alt":"Oberson’s Field at the Leetown battlefield with woods beyond the open ground","caption":"Osterhaus placed United States infantry along the south edge of Oberson’s Field. McCulloch’s planned attack would have emerged from the far tree line.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-3-leetown-battlefield.htm"},{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-leetown-field.jpg","alt":"Leetown battlefield and Oberson’s Field at Pea Ridge National Military Park","caption":"The open ground at Leetown. McCulloch and McIntosh were killed near the field’s north boundary.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-3-leetown-battlefield.htm"},{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-battle-print.jpg","alt":"1889 chromolithograph of fighting at Pea Ridge","caption":"Kurz & Allison’s 1889 print is a later artistic interpretation of the battle and includes American Indian troops.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645343/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 103","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/103-pea-ridge-part-the-third"},{"label":"National Park Service: Leetown Battlefield, March 7","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-3-leetown-battlefield.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Bentonville Detour","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/bentonville-detour.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Curtis’s headquarters","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/stop-2-pratt-s-store-general-curtis-s-headquarters.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Pea Ridge order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Ben McCulloch","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/general-mcculloch.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Louis Hébert","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/colonel-hebert.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Pea Ridge battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447263/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Battle of Pea Ridge print","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645343/"}]}}]}]
19:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Pea Ridge today",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame interactive-map-frame","children":["$","iframe",null,{"src":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-94.09%2C36.41%2C-93.89%2C36.52&layer=mapnik&marker=36.454%2C-94.034","title":"Pea Ridge today","loading":"lazy"}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Pea Ridge today"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The preserved battlefield lies northeast of Bentonville. Elkhorn Tavern is on the east side of the park; Leetown and Oberson’s Field are roughly two miles to the west."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":["$undefined",["$","a",null,{"href":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Map source ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Interactive map: drag to pan and use the +/− controls to zoom."}]]}]]}],["$","figure","How Van Dorn reached the United States rear",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/pea-ridge-bentonville-detour.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open How Van Dorn reached the United States rear at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/pea-ridge-bentonville-detour.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Bentonville Detour and the armies near Little Sugar Creek and Pea Ridge","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"How Van Dorn reached the United States rear"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The National Park Service map shows the Bentonville Detour around Curtis’s prepared line at Little Sugar Creek. Federal roadblocks delayed the Confederate column and contributed to Van Dorn’s decision to divide it."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/pea-ridge-bentonville-detour.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/bentonville-detour.htm","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Map source ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}],["$","figure","Battlefield roads and terrain",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/pea-ridge-battlefield-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Battlefield roads and terrain at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/pea-ridge-battlefield-map.jpg","alt":"1876 map of the Pea Ridge battlefield showing roads, settlements, terrain, and troop positions","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Battlefield roads and terrain"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"This 1876 U.S. Army map identifies the Telegraph Road, Elkhorn Tavern, Leetown, Cross Timber Hollow, the ridge, fields, and wooded ground. Its troop positions depict March 8, but the terrain and road network also explain the divided fighting on March 7."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/pea-ridge-battlefield-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],"$L21"]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
